{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PLAN REWRITTEN ACROSS 9 REVIEW ROUNDS — still BLOCK as of this comment (round 10 in flight). The\nplan is now materially different from the one this ticket started with, and the differences are the\npoint, so they are recorded here rather than left in the diff.\n\nWHAT THE ROUNDS ACTUALLY CAUGHT (each verified in source before I accepted it)\n\n 1. ROUND 1 (BLOCKING, G6) — the plan would have built a THIRD `Retry-After` implementation.\n    `_errors.parse_retry_after()` (`_errors.py:32`) and `_errors.MAX_BACKOFF_S = 60.0`\n    (`_errors.py:29`) already exist, `dispatch_one.py:41` already imports both, and\n    `dispatch_one.py:150-171` is ALREADY a 429-honouring retry path for the REST transport. The plan\n    now reuses them and cites that path as the sibling to read first.\n\n 2. ROUND 4 (BLOCKING, G6) — THE MOST IMPORTANT ONE. The plan routed 429 retries through\n    `_with_connection_retry`, which is the transport's single choke point for ALL 15 call sites,\n    INCLUDING `create_issue`/`add_comment`/`add_label`. Its docstring already states the policy and\n    the reason: JIRAError is unretried because \"retrying a mutation on an HTTP error risks\n    duplicates\". The plan as written would have REINTRODUCED THE DUPLICATE-ISSUE CLASS THAT\n    [rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] JUST FIXED. Now: 429 retry is scoped to IDEMPOTENT READS; mutations\n    keep today's fail-fast, because a 429 can arrive after the server began the write and nothing in\n    the response distinguishes that from rejection at the gate. The opt-in defaults to OFF so a\n    mutation added later cannot inherit it by omission.\n\n 3. ROUND 6 (BLOCKING) — the opt-in mechanism could not reach most of the reads. `_call_logged`\n    wraps `_with_connection_retry` (`:287`), and most reads route through it, so a parameter on the\n    inner wrapper alone was unreachable for them. `_call_logged` now forwards the same flag.\n\n 4. ROUND 6 also found `get_comment_map` has TWO network layers — besides the shared pager it\n    fetches per-issue comments at `:473` through a bare `_with_connection_retry`. That is ONE REQUEST\n    PER ISSUE, i.e. the highest-volume read in a pass and the likeliest thing to be rate limited, and\n    my \"three map readers covered by opting in one helper\" left exactly it uncovered.\n\n 5. ROUND 6/7 — `probe_remote` calls `_with_connection_retry` DIRECTLY (`:493`), so grouping it with\n    the `_call_logged` members made its opt-in structurally unreachable.\n\n 6. ROUND 9 — `transition_issue_by_name`'s READ leg (`transitions()`, `:373`) is a pure read I had\n    silently omitted. Fixing it sharpened the rule: THE BOUNDARY IS PER-CALL, NOT PER-METHOD — that\n    method's read leg opts in and its write leg (`:384`) does not.\n\nTWO REVIEWER CLAIMS I FIRST SCORED AS WRONG, AND WAS WRONG TO — both have the SAME root cause, and\nit is worth carrying forward: **the plan reviewer reads `origin/main`, not this story's base.**\n * \"`_paged_search` does not exist\" (raised 4x). It DOES — `transport.py:421` — but it arrives with\n   [rebar:9263-b404-cb6c-463f], which is in review as change 1129 and NOT on main\n   (`git show origin/main:...transport.py | grep -c _paged_search` -> 0). The finding was right about\n   the tree it reads. The plan now declares the 9263 dependency explicitly, with that command as the\n   check.\n * \"transport.py is 792 LOC, not 789\". BOTH are right: `origin/main` is 792, the post-9263 base is\n   789, and the 3-line delta IS 9263's pager consolidation. I rejected this in round 1 on a bare\n   `wc -l` of my own tree without noticing the reviewer reads a different one. The plan now names\n   both numbers and which base each applies to.\n\nMY OWN RECURRING FAILURE, recorded because it cost 3 of the 9 rounds: I fixed one section and left\nanother contradicting it (Scope vs Approach on `_call_logged`; an AC citing `:168` after the Approach\ncorrected it to `:169`). That is exactly the \"grep the WHOLE document for the term you changed\"\nlesson from handoff #4, and I did not apply it consistently.\n\nDESIGN DECISIONS NOW PINNED IN THE PLAN\n * Order of operations: jitter FIRST, clamp LAST — `min(MAX_BACKOFF_S, retry_after * (1 +\n   random.uniform(0, 0.2)))`. Clamp-last is what makes an oversized value land on exactly 60s;\n   jitter-last would give 60-72 and contradict the AC.\n * An oversized `Retry-After` is CLAMPED (matching `dispatch_one.py:166`), not a hard failure — a\n   second divergent policy for one signal is what the blocking finding was about.\n * `Retry-After: 0` parses to `0.0`, NOT None (`return secs if secs >= 0 else None`), so it is an\n   immediate retry bounded by the cap. An earlier draft claimed the helper returned None for 0.\n * The header is read from `exc.response.headers`, NOT `exc.headers`. Verified at runtime against\n   pycontribs 3.10.5: `JIRAError.__init__` takes `(text, status_code, url, request, response)` and\n   sets BOTH `self.response` and `self.headers` (the latter from **kwargs, typically None). Reading\n   the wrong one returns None even when the header is present — a silent always-falls-back failure.\n * Placement: `transport.py` is at the LOCKED 800 cap, so the retry/error cluster extracts to\n   `adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py` — and `TlsVerificationError` MUST move with it, or\n   `_tls_verification_error` (which instantiates it at `:218`) creates a circular import.\n   `transport.py` re-exports the moved names so `test_jira_dc_config_settings.py:183-184`, which\n   reaches both through the module object, keeps passing UNEDITED.\n\nNOT STARTED: no code has been written for this ticket. The plan must reach a certified attestation\nbefore it can be claimed."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785543361614506001,"uuid":"fd360ac7-e0a9-445b-82d1-6479c1a1b23c"}